# Detail + Briefing Overlay — LOCKED 30 Jul 2026

This is the single biggest piece of work in the initiative. It **supersedes** the 17 Jul 2026 wireframe (which had the detail page as a separate page with a second sidebar rail, two parallel submission cards, and a separate briefing page). The detail "page" is now an **overlay** over the Projects list; briefing is folded in as its Create mode.

## 1. The overlay shell

- **Near-fullscreen sheet** over the blurred Projects table (a small margin of blurred table visible around it; the global sidebar is blurred behind it too).
- **Close affordances:** X, click the blurred margin, Esc, and browser Back.
- **URL-addressable via a `project=<id>` query param** on the Projects page, managed with `history.pushState()`/`popstate`:
  - Clicking a row pushes `…?view=my&sort=…&project=123` (no reload), fetches the detail fragment, injects the overlay. Composes with the existing view/filter/sort/group params (the querystring is already the single source of truth — see `Projects.md`). Closing drops only `project=`, restoring the exact table state underneath.
  - A direct hit / refresh with `project=123` present auto-opens the overlay on load — so every inbound link (notifications, achievements, escalation flags, shared links) keeps working after the old `/projects/<id>` detail page is deleted.
  - An optional **"copy link"** control in the header, since links are now shareable.
- **Unsaved-edit guard:** closing (X / blur / Esc / Back) while Edit mode has unsaved changes prompts rather than discarding. `popstate` can't be truly cancelled, so the pattern is re-push the state and show the confirm.
- **Render-on-demand:** the table stays mounted underneath; detail content is fetched and injected on open, torn down on close.

## 2. Three modes: View / Edit / Create

The overlay is one component with three modes:
- **View** — read the project.
- **Edit** — a single **Edit toggle sticky with the project name at the top** flips the currently-visible section's brief fields editable at once, with one **Save / Cancel** for the session. CS/Management/Admin/**Project Owner** only. On Save: concurrent-edit check (nothing changed underneath since entering edit mode) → commit → SSE push to other viewers → **granular field-level activity log** (see §10). Edit mode governs brief *content* fields; interactive actions (team-tag self-assign, submissions, status transitions) stay always-on for their own gated roles regardless of edit mode. Deliverables keep their own dedicated edit mode (structural add/remove — the shared Briefing deliverables-edit component).
- **Create** — the edit-mode form + create-only controls (brief-type toggle Standard/C&CM, Project Owner assignment, production-only flag, generate Job/FOC number with the atomic fix, Review & Submit). On submit → switches straight to **View** of the new project — no page nav. **Drafts stay**, on the same modal (a draft = a project in Not Started; the drafts check is a step/list in the create flow).

## 3. The rail = the pipeline

The overlay's left rail is the pipeline itself, lighting up left-to-right as status advances:

**Design → Pre-Production → Production → Logistics**, plus **Finance** as an always-available cross-cutting tab.

- **Design** active from creation.
- **Pre-Production** activates at Client Approved (first-to-reach; see §4/§5).
- **Production** activates at Handed to Production.
- **Logistics** activates at Production Complete (future).

Tab activation is **first-to-reach, not all-to-reach** — the tab lights the moment the *first* deliverable reaches that stage, so nothing waits on the slowest deliverable. The same status transitions that move the pipeline also light the next tab — one state machine, not two.

Each active tab shows its **sub-sections as an indented accordion** beneath it (only one department's sub-sections expanded at a time). **Design's sub-sections: Project Details / Deliverables / Submissions** (Project Details is the first-visit default and always reachable since Design is always active; later visits default to Deliverables — needs the "has this user seen this project" marker table). Below the tabs: Project Files + Flag Issue (lighter weight), divider, Put on Hold + Cancel, then the close control.

**Role-gating matrix** (once a tab has content): Designer/Team Lead → Design + Pre-Production + Production · CS → all · Management → all · Project Owner → all (CS-equivalent) · Finance → all · Logistics → Logistics only · Production → Pre-Production + Production + Logistics. (Design/Pre-Prod/Production/Logistics/Finance/Project Owner are new `User.role` values — additive.)

## 4. The status model — three tiers

Each tier rolls up from the one below, in that tier's own vocabulary:

| Tier | Applies to | Vocabulary | Set by |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Deliverable status** (granular) | every deliverable, both brief types | Unassigned → In Progress → In Review (CS) → Submitted to Client → In Revision → Client Approved | **Derived** from assignment + latest submission state — not a separate manual field |
| **Pipeline status** | Standard *project* · C&CM *customer* | In Design → Client Approved → Pre-Production → Handed to Production (+ On Hold, Cancelled) | Advances as deliverables complete; Client Approved is CS-gated |
| **C&CM aggregate** (C&CM only) | the C&CM *project* overall | Briefed → In Progress → Design Completed | Computed from customers' pipeline states |

- **Standard** is a single deliverable-set, so its project-level status *is* the pipeline directly. **C&CM** gets the extra aggregate layer because it nests customers, each running their own pipeline: Briefed = created, no customer started · In Progress = at least one customer in flight · Design Completed = all customers reached Handed to Production.
- **Per-deliverable / per-customer status pills** render on every deliverable row and every C&CM customer header — the pill is the single source of truth for where that unit is.
- **Client Approved is a CS-gated action** with a **guarded confirmation modal** ("good to go" vs. "N deliverables still pending"). It's the summary milestone (all deliverables approved) — no longer the thing blocking individual deliverables from moving (see §5).
- **On Hold** reads the prior stage on resume (via the `ProjectStatusLog` `started_at`/`ended_at` bracketing already in place — the last non-hold row IS the prior state). Orthogonal to the pipeline.
- **"Approved" → "Client Approved"** rename; "Completed" is no longer the terminal blanket status — the pipeline continues past client sign-off.
- **This ripples to the Projects list page:** its Status column + Status filter (`_blanket_status()` and the `['Not Started','Active','On Hold','Completed']` list in `project_list.py`) get the new vocabulary. Done in the same rework so the two never disagree.

## 5. Per-deliverable pre-production — the single source of truth

The real workflow needs some client-approved deliverables to move into pre-production while others are still in design. **Gating at the project level would bottleneck; per-deliverable is the model.** It costs nothing extra in the backend because **the deliverable is one record that both phases read/write different fields on** — design status on one side, a pre-production layer (needs_technical / needs_artwork + each one's progress) on the other. No re-entry, no double data.

**What keeps the UI flawless — separate the *roster* from the *work-surfaces*:**
- **The Deliverables section (in Design) is the roster** — every deliverable, all phases, each with its status pill. You look here to answer "where is deliverable X?" for *any* deliverable. Nothing ever disappears from the roster.
- **Each phase tab is a work-surface, filtered to its phase.** Design's submission surface shows deliverables still in design; the Pre-Production tab shows only the ones that have crossed in (client-approved + Owner-flagged), with their technical/artwork work. A deliverable isn't "moved and lost" — it stays in the roster with a pill; its *active work surface* is whichever tab matches its phase.
- **Tabs activate first-to-reach** (§3), so pre-production opens as soon as the first deliverable is ready.
- **The project-level status is a summary rollup**, not a hard single-state gate.

The gate thus moves from project down to deliverable: each crosses its own Client Approved → Pre-Production line, Project-Owner-triggered, at its own pace. (Whether pre-production is allowed per-deliverable-early or only after the project-level Client Approved milestone is purely a question of *when the Owner may flip the flag* — the data model is identical either way, so nothing is locked out.)

## 6. Submissions flow

- **Design submissions are client-facing; technical/artwork files are internal production-prep.** The client approves the *look* (2D concepts, 3D renders); they never see DWG dielines or print-ready PDFs. So design submissions live In Design; technical/artwork live in Pre-Production.
- **Lifecycle:** Draft (always editable, optional non-blocking "Inform CS for Internal Review" ping) → **Submit to Client** (real permanent gate, with modified-since-notification check via SSE) → Sent to Client → [Approved | Revision Requested → Start Revision reopens Draft, no re-review]. Technical/artwork route mirrors this with "Mark Approved" as its terminal, feeding the **Handed to Production** transition (symmetry: design-approval feeds Client Approved, technical-completion feeds Handed to Production).
- **Only ONE active draft per channel.** Per-deliverable revision numbers auto-computed from history ("Initial" / "Revision N"). Two distinct labeled counts: Deliverable Revision Count vs Project/Customer Revision Count — never a bare number.
- **The deck is the invoicing record → the submission summary (locked 30 Jul 2026).** When CS clicks **Submit to Client** on any submission, a **submission summary modal** shows the *complete deck*: the deliverables newly going for decision, PLUS the ones already Client-Approved or in Pre-Production. The already-locked ones are **included in the deck (for client completeness + invoicing) but shown as read-only indicators — this submission never changes their status.** So CS always sees the whole scope and never loses track of what's locked vs. live. Reinforces the single-source model: an approved status is sticky and simply *displays* in every later deck it rides along in.
- **Deck versioning:** non-revision sends overwrite the same file in place; a confirmed revision creates a new preserved file. Expected deck filename previewed in draft mode. Project/Customer revision counter is separate, manual, CS-confirmed at send ("Is this a revision?").
- **Gulf per-customer submission routes:** each Gulf customer gets its own independent route (own draft/cards/counters) — a logic change, not schema (Gulf customers are already `ProjectCustomer` rows; `ProjectPosmChannel.posm_customer_id` is already nullable — always set it for Gulf too).
- **Universal undo principle:** every forward transition has a paired, visible, logged undo/reopen. Reserved for CS/Admin/Management/Owner: Sent → Draft; Revision Requested → Sent; Approved → prior state (asks whether to clean up auto-copied DWG/PDF files — cleanup can re-derive the path, likely no new column).
- **Deliverable picker** reuses the Deliverables roster as a select-for-submission mode, default-filtered to non-Approved (but see the summary above — approved ones still ride along in the deck as indicators).

## 7. Project Owner role (new)

- **New `User.role` value `project_owner`**, created in this pass. **CS-equivalent access** app-wide, but sits **above CS in the hierarchy** as the start-to-finish accountable owner.
- **Assigned in the brief** → new `Project.project_owner_id`, set at creation (coexists with CS Lead, doesn't replace it; nullable for existing/backfilled projects).
- On the move to Pre-Production, the Owner **gets notified, reviews, and selects which deliverables need technicals and/or artworks** (flipping the per-deliverable flags — incrementally, some now, others later).

## 8. Production-only projects

- A **Standard project**, flagged at creation, that **starts in Pre-Production** (Design skipped).
- CS fills a **Pre-Production requirements text box** and **adds the deliverables** directly (no design phase to generate them); those start already pre-production-flagged. CS **and** Project Owners can add/rename/remove them via edit-project.
- Project Owner still assigned. (That Pre-Production requirements text box exists in both cases — filled at creation for production-only, or when the phase opens for normal projects.)

## 9. Cancel + Archive

- **Cancel Project** (CS/Mgmt/Admin/Owner) — requires a **logged reason** (same pattern as the Pill Reset reason field), sets status **Cancelled**, moves the project out of active views into an **Archive view**. **Reversible** (reactivate) — history is preserved, and the cancellation is **visible on the dashboard to admin + management**.
- **Delete** (admin-only, from the archive) — the rare permanent removal, for genuine junk. This is where the soft-delete plan (`is_deleted`/`deleted_at`/`deleted_by_id`) lands.
- **Archive view** lists non-active projects (Cancelled, plus fully-completed down the line) with read-only view + reactivate.

## 10. Edit mode + granular logging + targeted notifications

- **Global-per-section edit** (§2): one top toggle, all the section's brief fields editable, single Save/Cancel, SSE push + concurrent-edit guard on Save.
- **Granular field-level logging:** on Save, **diff before vs. after and log each changed field** — "Initial Deadline: 18 Jun → 24 Jun," "Deliverable 'Wobbler' added," "Deliverable 'Standee' removed," each with actor + timestamp — replacing today's flat "project edited by X." Upgrades `log_activity` to field-level detail; feeds the combined timeline (system activity + Project Notes).
- **Deliverable add/remove fires targeted notifications:** on add, notify designers whose team is requested for it; on remove, notify whoever was assigned — so designers hear about scope changes to their work, with the what and when.

## 11. Project Notes + Site Visits — the outlier layer

The system deliberately does NOT model every workflow outlier (that way lies spaghetti). Instead:
- **Project Notes** — freeform, attributed, timestamped, optional file link, optional **tags**: *"Ibrahim — early draft of 1×1 stand technical — 4 Aug 10AM — [link]."* The general escape-hatch for out-of-scope work; tags set up the future chat features. Distinct from the automatic activity log (machine-written) — these are human-written annotations, though they can render in one combined timeline.
- **Site Visits** — a **structured** record (technical person, project, date, start/end times, optional location/notes), specifically so the **dashboard can show CS/management when a technical designer is out of the building**. Structured (not a freeform note) precisely because the dashboard needs the dates/times to compute availability. Also documents the visit on the project timeline. (This is how early technical work — site visits, early drafts — is captured *without* unlocking Pre-Production early, except on production-only projects.)

## 12. Design tab sub-section content (carried from the 17 Jul wireframe where still valid)

- **Project Details** — four cards: Project Name (Job Number, Status, Add Secondary CS, CS Lead, Secondary CS, **Project Owner**), Designer Assignments (Team/Designer/Action), Brief Overview (Client, Initial/Final Deadline, Client Expectation / What to Avoid / Additional Info — rich text), Reference Files (drag-drop anywhere on the card; per-file Preview/Download/Delete, Delete = CS/Admin/Mgmt/Owner).
- **Deliverables (the roster)** — one row each: name (left), 2D/3D/Technical team tags → Deadline → Files → Status pill (right). Header: count + Focused/All toggle (default Focused; team-scoped roles only — CS/Mgmt/Admin/Owner see All). Team-tag **click-to-assign** inline (dashed "+" affordance at rest; designers self-assign, team leads assign their team; reassignment confirm-gated). Files button = deliverable's NAS working folder.
- **C&CM Deliverables** — Region → Customer → Deliverable nesting; two independent collapse levels (region, customer); Focused/All at region level; count at both levels; Install Date on the customer header; bulk self-assign (per-customer Assign Multiple / Assign to All, per-region Assign me to region) with conflict modal.

---

## Sitewide Density / Sizing System (locked 17 Jul 2026 — built)

Every density-scaling size (fonts, padding, gaps, avatar diameter, row height) is in `rem` off the root `<html>` font-size, so one root value scales the whole app's density. Not a user-facing toggle. The token layer (`main.css` `:root`) is live — reuse it for the overlay, don't invent new values.

---

## Build Process / File Organization

- **Fresh files, never edits to the old ones.** Old files (`projects_detail.py` and siblings, `detail.js`, `create.html`) are read as reference only; new code goes in new files; old files get deleted in the same pass that cuts the overlay over.
- **Comment standard non-negotiable** — what/how/why per block.
- **File granularity for the overlay:** the Detail+Briefing overlay is one coherent surface. Suggested split: a `project_overlay` blueprint + template + one CSS file, with JS split by concern (shell/routing, view, edit, create, submissions, pre-production) so a given interaction only loads what it needs. Finance/Production/Logistics tab JS/CSS created only when that tab gets real content, not pre-stubbed.
- **`STATIC_VERSION` cache-busting:** restart Flask after any static-asset edit or changes won't show.

